{
  "image_name": "0b3ece373c027b93465f449b16704911.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes two common ways to study gene function in early embryos and the developmental consequences that can be tested. At the top, it contrasts a standard zygotic knockout with a maternal-plus-zygotic knockout. In the zygotic knockout, a heterozygous cross produces a zygote that may inherit a knockout allele, but the egg still contains maternally supplied protein and/or mRNA from the wild-type mother. This means early development can be buffered by maternal gene products. In the maternal-and-zygotic knockout, both the oocyte and sperm carry knockout alleles, so the embryo lacks both maternal stores and zygotic gene activity, revealing earlier or stronger phenotypes.\n\nThe lower part shows an embryo-manipulation approach beginning at the 2-cell stage. One blastomere is injected with GFP together with mRNA or siRNA, creating a labeled clone that can be followed as the embryo develops to the 4-cell stage and then to the blastocyst. The final drawings suggest comparing where the manipulated cells end up within the blastocyst, apparently asking whether the treatment changes cell fate or positioning, such as contribution to inner versus outer cell populations. Overall, the figure illustrates strategies for separating maternal and zygotic gene effects and testing lineage outcomes in preimplantation embryos.",
